# Copy to .env and adjust. .env is gitignored.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Two kinds of value live here. compose.yml reads some of them (paths, ports,
# image tags) when containers start; configure.sh and the timer scripts read the
# rest and push them into the running apps. Changing one of those needs
# ./configure.sh afterwards - the README's ".env settings" notes say which.
#
# Sections, in order:
#   1 Identity and paths        6 Usenet provider          11 Automation timers
#   2 Network and hostnames     7 Seeding and bandwidth    12 Dashboard extras
#   3 Plex                      8 Quality and the guard    13 Compose overrides
#   4 Web UI login              9 The VPN (required)       14 Image tags
#   5 Language and dates       10 Alerts


# ===========================================================================
# 1. Identity and paths
# ===========================================================================
# The user the containers run as. Must OWN ./config and ./data. Run `id -u` and
# `id -g` - do not assume 1000/1000, the primary group id often differs from the
# user id.
PUID=1000
PGID=1000

# Files the containers create are group-writable (rw-rw-r--, rwxrwxr-x).
UMASK=002

# Every container's clock, as an IANA zone - setup.sh reads this host's. It is
# what timestamps a log line, when a nightly search or an update window falls,
# and the local time an app shows next to an air date.
TZ=Etc/UTC

# Relative to this directory, or absolute. What matters for hardlinks is that
# everything under DATA_ROOT - torrents, usenet and media - sits on ONE
# filesystem. CONFIG_ROOT may live anywhere.
DATA_ROOT=./data
CONFIG_ROOT=./config


# ===========================================================================
# 2. Network and hostnames
# ===========================================================================

# Interface the apps' own UI ports (8989, 7878, ...) bind to. 127.0.0.1 keeps
# them off the LAN - Caddy hostnames are the way in. Set 0.0.0.0 to also allow
# http://<ip>:<port> from other devices.
APP_BIND=127.0.0.1

# This host's LAN IP. setup.sh fills it; used for the dashboard's allowed hosts.
LAN_IP=192.168.1.2

# Domain suffix for the service hostnames: sonarr.<SITE_DOMAIN>,
# media.<SITE_DOMAIN>, ... Your DNS server must resolve them to LAN_IP (README
# "URLs, hostnames and ports"). home.arpa is the RFC 8375 name for home
# networks; use your own if you have one.
SITE_DOMAIN=home.arpa


# ===========================================================================
# 3. Plex
# ===========================================================================
# Networks Plex treats as local (full-speed direct play, no remote caps): this
# LAN, any other site's LAN, and the Tailscale range if you use it. setup.sh
# fills in the local subnet and asks for the others.
PLEX_LAN_NETWORKS=192.168.1.0/24

# Certification (content-rating) country for the Plex libraries and Seerr's
# discover region, ISO 3166-1 alpha-2, e.g. US, NL, DE, GB.
PLEX_CERTIFICATION_COUNTRY=US

# Public port for Plex remote access over the internet: forward this TCP port
# on your internet router to LAN_IP:32400. Empty = keep Plex off the internet
# (VPN only). Note that remote access publishes this address to plex.tv and to
# everyone you share a library with.
# Set, it also turns Plex's own Relay fallback off (it is capped low enough to
# be a playback problem) and makes heal.sh watch the mapping, so a forward that
# stops working reaches you as a `health` alert instead of silently.
PLEX_PUBLIC_PORT=32400

# Size ceiling for Plex's in-RAM transcoding scratch (compose mounts it as a
# tmpfs at /transcode). tmpfs occupies only what is written, so this is a cap
# and not a reservation, but it must stay well under total RAM: Linux allows a
# tmpfs at most half of it by default. Roughly 2G per simultaneous transcode.
PLEX_TRANSCODE_RAM=8g

# One-time claim token from https://www.plex.tv/claim (valid 4 minutes).
# Only needed on the very first start; leave empty afterwards.
PLEX_CLAIM=


# ===========================================================================
# 4. Web UI login
# ===========================================================================
# One login for qBittorrent, Sonarr, Radarr, Lidarr, Prowlarr, Bazarr and
# SABnzbd. configure.sh asks on its first run (Enter = generate a password),
# stores them here and applies them everywhere. Not read by compose.yml.
# Leave the password empty to be prompted.
WEBUI_USERNAME=admin
WEBUI_PASSWORD=


# ===========================================================================
# 5. Language, dates and dubbing
# ===========================================================================
# Subtitle languages for Bazarr's default profile (ISO 639-1 codes, in order
# of preference). Created once; edit later in Bazarr's UI.
SUBTITLE_LANGUAGES=en

# Dates and clock in the web UIs and on the dashboard. The UI language stays
# English; these only set the formats. UI_LOCALE is the switch: empty or en-US
# leaves Sonarr, Radarr, Lidarr and Prowlarr at their own defaults, anything
# else applies the three formats below and gives the dashboard its clock in that
# locale (nl-NL: 24-09-2026, 13:40).
UI_LOCALE=nl-NL
# One of YYYY-MM-DD (2026-09-24, sorts), DD/MM/YYYY or MM/DD/YYYY. The long
# date and the calendar's week column follow from it.
UI_DATE_FORMAT=YYYY-MM-DD
# 24h or 12h - the apps and the dashboard clock.
UI_TIME_FORMAT=24h
# monday or sunday, for the calendar and the week numbers.
UI_FIRST_DAY_OF_WEEK=monday

# A second audio language to prefer, beside the original (ISO 639-1 code).
# Empty is the default and creates nothing at all. Set it and every quality
# profile in Radarr and Sonarr starts preferring one file that carries both the
# original audio and this language - the case for young children, who need the
# audio translated because subtitles do not help them yet, without a second
# copy for everyone else. Nothing is required and nothing is refused for
# lacking it: a film with no dub available downloads exactly as before.
# There is one library, one profile per quality and one request.
# pt also accepts Portuguese (Brazil) and es also accepts Spanish (Latino),
# which the apps count as languages of their own; nl, pt, sv, tr and cs get an
# extra format for the tag spellings their parser reads as Unknown.
# README "Dubbed audio (the original language plus one more)".
DUB_LANGUAGE=


# ===========================================================================
# 6. Usenet provider
# ===========================================================================
# Your own account, for SABnzbd. Leave empty to skip. Port 563 = TLS (default),
# 119 = plain.
USENET_HOST=
USENET_PORT=563
USENET_USERNAME=
USENET_PASSWORD=
USENET_CONNECTIONS=8

# Spotweb, a Spotnet indexer that Prowlarr searches as a Newznab source. It
# reuses the account above for its own header retrieval, so there is nothing
# else to type. configure.sh sets it up, closes public registration and
# registers it in Prowlarr.
#
# How often it retrieves new headers, in minutes. Longer than a run takes, or
# two runs overlap - the first pass on an empty database scans the whole group
# and takes several minutes. 0 leaves retrieval off.
SPOTWEB_INTERVAL_MIN=30

# How far back to index, as a year. Spots older than this are read but not
# stored, which is what keeps the first retrieval from importing the whole
# archive. Spotnet spots start around 2010, so no earlier year finds more.
#
# How far back is worth indexing depends on your Usenet provider's article
# retention, not on Spotweb: a spot older than your provider's window will index
# fine and download nothing. Retention differs per provider and generally grows
# over time, so check what yours offers.
#
# Changing this later does not re-index by itself - Spotweb separately tracks
# how far it has read, and only moves forward. Run
# ./scripts/spotweb-backfill.sh afterwards, which reads this same key. See
# README "Spotweb, the Spotnet indexer".
SPOTWEB_SINCE_YEAR=2025

# Alert when no new spot has arrived for this many hours - retrieval fails
# silently otherwise, because Spotweb reports a crash and still exits 0.
# Needs `disk`-style alerts on: see NTFY_EVENTS. 0 turns the check off.
SPOTWEB_STALE_HOURS=6

# Series whose releases are posted without any quality in the title - Spotweb
# has a steady trickle of these, usually season packs written in prose - listed
# by TVDB id, comma-separated, no spaces. They get their own copy of the default
# quality profile that also accepts Sonarr's "Unknown", so the episode is no
# longer found and then discarded.
#
# Per series on purpose: allowing Unknown on the shared profile would accept
# anything unparseable from every indexer. Find an id in the series URL in
# Sonarr, or on thetvdb.com. Empty = nothing gets the exception.
# 255602 = Ik Vertrek
SONARR_ALLOW_UNKNOWN_TVDBIDS=255602


# ===========================================================================
# 7. Seeding and bandwidth
# ===========================================================================
# qBittorrent seeding limits. A torrent counts as finished once either is
# reached; Sonarr/Radarr then remove it (Remove Completed). 0 = unlimited.
TORRENT_SEED_RATIO=2
TORRENT_SEED_DAYS=7

# Private trackers usually call a torrent a hit and run unless it seeded a
# minimum time, whatever ratio it reached - and a ratio cap would stop it long
# before that, since ratio 1 arrives after about three hours here. So indexers
# Prowlarr marks private get this seed time and no ratio cap, while public ones
# fall back on TORRENT_SEED_RATIO / TORRENT_SEED_DAYS above. 0 treats private
# trackers like public ones. Check your tracker's own rules before lowering it.
TORRENT_PRIVATE_SEED_HOURS=72

# Rate caps in KiB/s, so the rest of the house keeps bandwidth. KiB rather than
# Mbit because that is the unit both clients store: the number here is applied
# exactly, where a Mbit value would be rounded down to the nearest KiB (100
# Mbit/s is 12207.03 KiB, so it landed 32 B/s short). To convert your line
# speed: KiB = Mbit x 125000 / 1024, near enough Mbit x 122. The values below
# are 600 / 200 / 100 Mbit. On a 1 Gbit line that leaves at least 200 Mbit in
# the worst case and usually far more, since torrents (through the VPN) seldom
# use their share. 0 = unlimited.
USENET_MAX_KIB=73242
TORRENT_MAX_KIB=24414
TORRENT_UPLOAD_MAX_KIB=12207

# How many torrents qBittorrent works on at once; everything else it queues.
# qBittorrent's own defaults are 3/3/5, which with a few hundred torrents leaves
# almost all of them queued and looking stalled. Both halves matter. A download
# slot held by a torrent with no seeders blocks the queue behind it indefinitely,
# so a thin swarm stops being everyone else's problem once there are more slots
# than dead torrents. And a completed torrent cannot reach TORRENT_SEED_RATIO
# without an upload slot, which is what Sonarr and Radarr wait for before
# removing it - so the backlog grows faster than it drains.
# TORRENT_MAX_KIB and TORRENT_UPLOAD_MAX_KIB still bound the line: more slots
# buys parallelism, not more bandwidth, and each active torrent gets less.
TORRENT_MAX_ACTIVE_DOWNLOADS=8
TORRENT_MAX_ACTIVE_UPLOADS=10
TORRENT_MAX_ACTIVE_TOTAL=20

# Lifts every cap in that window (HH:MM-HH:MM, daily); empty = capped around
# the clock.
UNRESTRICTED_HOURS=01:00-06:00


# ===========================================================================
# 8. Quality and the release guard
# ===========================================================================
# What a film or episode should be, by default. All four of these exist as a
# quality profile in Radarr and in Sonarr, so a single title can always be
# asked for differently, in Seerr under "Advanced" or in the app itself:
#
#   1080p-encode   1080p, an encode of the disc     4-20 GB
#   1080p-remux    1080p, the disc untouched        15 GB and up
#   2160p-encode   2160p ("4K"), an encode          15-60 GB
#   2160p-remux    2160p, the disc untouched        30 GB and up
#
# The profiles themselves come from the TRaSH Guides, synced by Recyclarr: they
# pick the best release available rather than the smallest, and they allow
# upgrades, so a WEB-DL is replaced when a Bluray-sourced release appears. This
# value is only the default - what Seerr asks with, and what a film or series on
# one of the apps' own stock profiles is moved onto. Anything already on one of
# the four (or on a dubbed twin) stays where it is. 2160p is what "4K" means
# here; 8K has no quality tier in Radarr or Sonarr at all.
MEDIA_QUALITY=1080p-encode

# The profile Seerr hands new requests to. Empty = the default above; a name
# here has to exist in Radarr, and an unknown one falls back to its first.
SEERR_QUALITY_PROFILE=

# How far a downloaded file's duration may differ from the published runtime
# before the guard calls it a fake: 0.90 = at least 90% of the runtime,
# 1.15 = at most 115%. Measured against 49 imports here, genuine files land
# within 2% of the runtime and the one fake came in at 50%, so the window is
# deliberately narrow. The cost is alternate cuts: an extended or director's
# cut runs 1.1 to 1.4 times the theatrical runtime TMDB publishes, and the
# guard will reject and blocklist every copy of one. Raise the ceiling if you
# want those - the log says "REJECTED ... ratio 1.2x" when it happens.
# How far a file's duration may sit from the runtime TVDB or TMDb publishes
# before the guard calls it a fake, as a ratio of expected.
#
# The floor is 0.80 rather than something tighter because the two sources do not
# always measure the same thing: for an ad-supported broadcast the metadata is
# often the SLOT, while the file is the programme. A 55 minute slot arrives as a
# 45 minute file - ratio 0.82 - and a tighter floor throws those away. Dutch
# commercial channels do this routinely. Public-broadcast metadata tends to be
# content length already, which is why the same guard measures 0.99 to 1.01 on
# those. Real fakes sit far lower: the one caught here ran at 0.50.
#
# The ceiling catches a file that is too long for what it claims. It also
# rejects alternate cuts - an extended or director's cut runs 1.1 to 1.4x the
# Debugging the release guard, both off by default.
# GUARD_DRY_RUN=1 makes it print the decision it would take and then take none -
# the way to see why a grab was refused without changing the outcome.
# GUARD_EXPECTED_MINUTES overrides the runtime it compares a file against,
# instead of the one TMDb gives, for a title whose metadata is simply wrong.
GUARD_DRY_RUN=0
GUARD_EXPECTED_MINUTES=

# theatrical runtime - so raise it if you want those.
GUARD_MIN_RATIO=0.80
GUARD_MAX_RATIO=1.15


# ===========================================================================
# 9. The VPN - REQUIRED
# ===========================================================================
# README "Torrents through a VPN (Gluetun)". qBittorrent, Prowlarr and
# FlareSolverr run inside Gluetun's network namespace and have no other route
# out, so without a key they do not start and nothing is searched or downloaded.
# Proton VPN: in your account, generate a WireGuard configuration with
# "NAT-PMP (Port Forwarding)" ticked and copy its PrivateKey here (Plus plan;
# the key works on every server).
VPN_WIREGUARD_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Countries as Gluetun names them; the nearest one is the fastest.
VPN_SERVER_COUNTRIES=Netherlands


# ===========================================================================
# 10. Alerts (ntfy)
# ===========================================================================
# The server grants nobody access by default, so the topic can be named for
# what it is: configure.sh creates the account below, generating the password on
# its first run, and points every app at it. Subscribe on a phone with the ntfy
# app: server http://ntfy.<SITE_DOMAIN>, the topic and the same login.
# Reachable on the LAN and over Tailscale, not from the internet - a phone off
# both gets what it missed when it reconnects.
# Empty NTFY_TOPIC = no alerts configured anywhere.
NTFY_TOPIC=media-stack
NTFY_USER=media-stack
NTFY_PASSWORD=

# What to be told about. Comma-separated, any of:
#   ready   - a film or episode finished importing and can be played now
#   failed  - a download failed and nothing was left to try
#   manual  - a download is stuck and needs a human
#   health  - an app reports a problem, and when it recovers
#   disk    - free space crossed a mark, or the brakes went on
#   grab    - a download started
#   upgrade - a better version replaced a file you already had
# The names are deliberately app-neutral: the apps disagree, and one of them
# has two events that sound the same but are not. Sonarr fires "ready" once per
# download, not once per episode, so a 24-episode season pack is one alert.
# Empty = the connections exist but say nothing.
NTFY_EVENTS=ready,failed,manual,health,disk

# Which apps send ready, failed, grab and upgrade. health and manual always
# come from all of them - an app left out here can still say it is broken.
# Music imports are per track, which is why lidarr is not in the default.
NTFY_EVENT_APPS=sonarr,radarr

# How loudly, per kind of alert. 1 = min, 2 = low, 3 = default, 4 = high,
# 5 = max. Only 4 and 5 override a phone's do-not-disturb; 1 and 2 arrive
# without a sound. Use the same names as NTFY_EVENTS; anything not named here
# gets 4. A single number instead of a list sets every kind to that level.
# One connection per distinct level is created in each app, because an app
# carries one level per connection - so nothing here is free-form, but nothing
# is shared either.
NTFY_PRIORITY=ready:2,failed:5,manual:4,health:3,disk:5

# The disk. A full filesystem does not degrade gracefully - journald, Docker
# and every container fail together - so there are brakes, and they all come
# off this one number, in whole GiB.
#
# At DISK_FLOOR_GIB: SABnzbd pauses its queue and resumes by itself, the *arrs
# heal.sh stops downloading torrents. Imports are not held back: on one
# filesystem they cost nothing, and importing is what frees the download folder.
# Seeding is left running; it costs no space. qBittorrent has no free-space
# setting of its own, so it is stopped on a poll, which is why torrents
# actually stop a little above the floor - by whatever TORRENT_MAX_KIB can
# pull in one HEAL_INTERVAL, at least 2 GiB. The floor itself is what holds.
#
# Set it above the largest single download you expect to finish, plus room for
# an unpack and a container image pull, or an in-flight file walks straight
# through it. 25 is a sensible floor on a large disk; 5 protects almost
# nothing. 0 turns every brake off.
DISK_FLOOR_GIB=25

# Where to be told, while it is still easy to fix. Warns once on the way down
# and once back above it with a 10 percent margin, and names how much the next
# orphan sweep would free. 0 turns the warning off.
DISK_WARN_GIB=75

# How much of the library should live in the cloud rather than on the disk, as
# a share of the whole - local plus already archived, so the denominator does
# not shrink as the mover works and leave the figure chasing its own tail. 100
# puts everything eligible there and keeps the disk as a staging area; 50 keeps
# half local; 0 turns the share policy off and leaves only the emergency below.
#
# It is a placement policy, not a free-space target, so the mover runs whenever
# the share is short - not only when the disk is filling. Two things still bound
# it: ARCHIVE_MIN_AGE_DAYS, and a title whose file is still hardlinked to a
# seeding torrent, which is skipped because moving it uploads a copy to the
# cloud while the torrent's copy holds the disk anyway.
#
# DISK_WARN_GIB remains the emergency. Below it the mover moves whatever
# ARCHIVE_MAX_GIB_PER_RUN allows whether the share is met or not, because a disk
# about to fill is a worse problem than a library in the wrong place. The floor
# is where downloading stops - SABnzbd pauses, torrents stop - so the warn mark
# has to come first or the damage it exists to prevent has already been done.
ARCHIVE_REMOTE_PERCENT=100

# Ceiling on how much one mover run may upload. Four reasons it is not
# unlimited, and only the first actually binds at these numbers:
#
#   * rclone's cache. Everything a run moves is written there before it
#     uploads, and a file still waiting to upload is the one thing rclone will
#     not evict. So this has to fit RCLONE_CACHE_MAX - half of it, not all,
#     because vfs-cache-max-age is an hour and the timer fires hourly, so two
#     runs can be resident at once. mover.sh clamps to half if this is set
#     higher, rather than refusing: a disk filling is the worse outcome.
#   * the provider's daily cap. Google stops accepting uploads after 750 GiB in
#     a day; at 25 GiB across five quiet hours that is a sixth of it.
#   * the app's task queue. The move is synchronous inside Sonarr or Radarr and
#     holds that queue while it runs, so imports wait behind it - about three
#     minutes per run on a gigabit line, twice that at 50.
#   * the line. 25 GiB is minutes uncapped; the quiet window would fit far more
#     runs than the hourly timer will ever start.
ARCHIVE_MAX_GIB_PER_RUN=25

# Nothing added more recently than this is a candidate, however full the disk
# gets. Recent arrivals are the ones most likely to be watched next.
ARCHIVE_MIN_AGE_DAYS=90

# Smallest file the mover will send to the cloud, in MiB. Subtitles, posters and
# .nfo files cost nothing on the local disk and Bazarr rewrites subtitles in
# place, so sending them to the archive buys no space and turns every write into
# a round trip. 0 archives everything. README "The archive tier".
ARCHIVE_MIN_FILE_MIB=64

# What the archive upload may take, as an rclone bandwidth timetable. The stack
# caps every other bulk mover - USENET_MAX_KIB down, TORRENT_UPLOAD_MAX_KIB up -
# and without this one the archive is the only uncapped thing on the line: a
# mover run takes the whole uplink and Plex streams out and torrent seeding go
# with it.
#
# Format is rclone's own: "HH:MM,RATE HH:MM,RATE", and the rates are binary, so
# 30517K is 250 Mbit/s (Mbit x 125000 / 1024, the same arithmetic as the two
# keys above). "off" means no limit.
#
# The hours here must match UNRESTRICTED_HOURS - compose cannot split that value
# and rclone wants a timetable, so this is the one place the window is written
# twice. Change both together or the archive keeps uploading at full rate into
# the hours the rest of the stack is throttled for.
ARCHIVE_UPLOAD_WINDOW="06:00,30517K 01:00,off"

# Ceiling on rclone's local read cache for the archive. It lives on the same
# disk as everything else, so it competes with DISK_FLOOR_GIB - the floor and
# this together must leave room to work in.
RCLONE_CACHE_MAX=50G


# ===========================================================================
# 11. Automation timers
# ===========================================================================
# Each value ends up in a systemd user timer when you run the matching
# ./<script>.sh install - change one and run that command again.

# How often heal.sh looks for unhealthy containers (a systemd time span).
HEAL_INTERVAL=2min

# How often watch.sh looks. It only reads - container health, free space on the
# apps' side, whether Spotweb is still retrieving, whether Plex still has its
# port mapping and the archive its mount - so it does not need heal.sh's two
# minutes, and asking plex.tv that often would be hundreds of pointless calls a
# day. A systemd time span: 30s, 10min, 1h.
WATCH_INTERVAL=10min
# How long a magnet may sit without metadata before it is given up on. A magnet
# whose swarm has nobody holding the metadata never resolves, and nothing else
# in the stack will notice: qBittorrent's timers cover slow and inactive
# *seeding* rather than this, Sonarr and Radarr have no stalled-download
# handling at all, and the state is neither failed nor finished, so heal.sh's
# other evictions skip it. It still counts as an active download and holds one
# of the client's queue slots - three of them once stopped 38 healthy torrents
# from starting at all.
#
# The release is blocklisted on the way out and the app searches for another, so
# this costs a re-search rather than the episode. 0 turns the check off.
HEAL_METADATA_STALL_MINUTES=60



# Reclaiming abandoned downloads. An upgrade or a release-guard rejection
# deletes the library file while the download client keeps its own copy, and
# when the torrent later leaves the client that copy is left behind - owned by
# nothing and growing with every upgrade. heal.sh deletes such a file when it
# has no library link, no torrent covers it, every app's queue is empty and
# nothing has touched it for this many hours. 0 turns the sweep off.
HEAL_ORPHAN_HOURS=24
# Categories under data/torrents it never touches, comma-separated (no spaces:
# heal.sh reads this file with bash). What you fetched by hand through Prowlarr
# is yours to sort, not the stack's to delete.
# Applies to both sweeps: the torrent tree and SABnzbd's complete folder. These
# two categories are where a manual grab lands - from Prowlarr's own Search page,
# or a hand-picked album - and nothing imports those, so they would otherwise be
# reclaimed out from under you. The cost of protecting them is that they
# accumulate: drop prowlarr from this list if you grab by hand often and would
# rather the sweep clear up after you.
HEAL_ORPHAN_KEEP=prowlarr,music

# How often news.sh refreshes the dashboard headlines.
NEWS_INTERVAL=15min

# When missing.sh asks Radarr and Sonarr to search what is still missing
# (HH:MM; inside UNRESTRICTED_HOURS is the cheapest).
MISSING_SEARCH_TIME=02:00

# When update.sh pulls new images: a systemd weekday and a time.
UPDATE_DAY=Sun
UPDATE_TIME=04:00

# Recyclarr: the TRaSH Guides sync (quality definitions, custom formats and the
# guides' own quality profiles). How often it re-syncs, in cron syntax or one
# of @daily / @weekly / @hourly.
RECYCLARR_SCHEDULE=@daily


# ===========================================================================
# 12. Dashboard extras
# ===========================================================================
# Homepage at http://media.<SITE_DOMAIN>. Everything in this section is
# optional: an empty value leaves that tile or widget out. Applied by
# configure.sh, which regenerates the dashboard files on every run. The
# Infrastructure row itself is always there - Recyclarr, FlareSolverr and the
# Docker proxy need no configuration.

# Pi-hole tiles (queries, blocked, blocklist size), up to two: the address of a
# Pi-hole v6 web interface, its password or better an app password (Settings >
# Web interface / API), and the tile's title. A second one, e.g. at another site
# reached over Tailscale, goes in PIHOLE2_*. Quote titles with spaces
# ("Pi-hole Amsterdam"); an empty title falls back to "Pi-hole". No URL, no
# tile. Prefer a hostname over an IP: a tile's link is opened by your browser,
# which may not reach an address the host can. Secrets: this file stays mode 600.
PIHOLE_URL=
PIHOLE_PASSWORD=
PIHOLE_LABEL=
PIHOLE2_URL=
PIHOLE2_PASSWORD=
PIHOLE2_LABEL=

# Weather in the header, from Open-Meteo (no account, no key): decimal
# coordinates and the name shown next to them. Uses TZ above.
WEATHER_LATITUDE=
WEATHER_LONGITUDE=
WEATHER_LABEL=

# Headline tiles: "Title|URL" pairs separated by semicolons, any RSS or Atom
# feed. Several URLs after one title (space-separated) are merged into one
# tile, newest of each source in turn. news.sh fetches them (every 15 minutes
# once installed with ./news.sh install) and the dashboard reads the results
# from its own container, so the feeds never see the browser. Empty, the
# default, means no headline tiles at all. Each tile takes as many sources as
# you like; quote the value, the scripts source this file with bash:
#   NEWS_FEEDS="NOS|https://feeds.nos.nl/nosnieuwsalgemeen;Tech|https://www.techzine.nl/feed/ https://tweakers.net/feeds/nieuws.xml"
NEWS_FEEDS=


# ===========================================================================
# 13. Compose overrides and GPU
# ===========================================================================
# Colon-separated. Uncomment ONE line and add what you use.
#   compose.hwaccel.amd.yml     Plex and Jellyfin transcoding, Intel/AMD (VA-API via /dev/dri)
#   compose.hwaccel.nvidia.yml  Plex and Jellyfin transcoding, NVIDIA (needs nvidia-container-toolkit)
# Plex hardware transcoding needs Plex Pass. The VPN is not an override - it is
# part of compose.yml and cannot be switched off.
#COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yml:compose.hwaccel.amd.yml
#COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yml:compose.hwaccel.nvidia.yml

# Opt-in services, comma-separated. Empty means none of them run.
#   archive   the rclone mount that /data/archive comes from, so the library
#             can overflow somewhere else when this disk fills. Turned on by
#             scripts/archive-setup.sh once a remote exists - see README
#             "The archive tier". Everything else carries on without it: the
#             apps simply have no second root folder.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=

# Group ids that own /dev/dri/* on the host, used by compose.hwaccel.amd.yml.
# Check with: getent group render video
RENDER_GID=989
VIDEO_GID=985


# ===========================================================================
# 14. Image tags
# ===========================================================================
# `latest` + explicit `docker compose pull` is the upgrade strategy (README
# "Updating containers"). To roll back, pin a tag from the image's release page,
# e.g. SONARR_TAG=4.0.15.2941-ls290
HOMEPAGE_TAG=latest
NTFY_TAG=latest
PLEX_TAG=latest
JELLYFIN_TAG=latest
SONARR_TAG=latest
RADARR_TAG=latest
LIDARR_TAG=latest
PROWLARR_TAG=latest
BAZARR_TAG=latest
SEERR_TAG=latest
SABNZBD_TAG=latest
SPOTWEB_TAG=latest
RCLONE_TAG=latest
QBITTORRENT_TAG=latest
FLARESOLVERR_TAG=latest
GLUETUN_TAG=latest
DOCKERPROXY_TAG=latest
# Recyclarr pins to a major version: its config format changes between them.
RECYCLARR_TAG=8
